The client socket was the stderr of the process of a session: what a library vsftpd
calls into prints there (an NSS module such as NIS, a PAM module, libwrap) went into
the FTP control connection, confusing the client and showing it the internals of the
server. standalone.c no longer puts the socket on fd 2 (the session inherits the
stderr of the listener: /dev/null with background=YES); main.c points fd 1 and 2 at
/dev/null when they are the socket (inetd) or closed, before any NSS, PAM or libwrap
code runs. vsftpd itself talks to the client on fd 0 only.
From openSUSE (vsftpd 3.0.5, vsftpd-no-stderr-on-control-connection.patch, Peter
Simons, bsc#1184651), on vsftpd-ext 3.0.3.1: the same code, the hunk of main.c after
its standalone block (vsftpd-ext has no die_init() there). vsftpd-ext and
vsftpd-ext-openssl carry the same file. Tested on Linux by the vsftpd part of
tools/servers-test.sh (all its checks pass).

